{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/1469460-purpose-and-the-chronicle","id":1469460,"title":"Purpose and the Chronicle","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-12-02T19:36:14-08:00","id":1469460,"title":"Purpose and the Chronicle","content":"So I have a question for you Mummy Storytellers out there. Purpose, the reason behind why a Mummy has awakened, by its nature plays an important part in the life of the Mummy while it walks the Earth and the player that portrays them. That being said, I was curious to hear what your preferred &quot;type&quot; of Purpose is and how large a role it plays in the Chronicle at large.<br /> <br /> I mean this in two respects. First, the type of Purpose. We all know about the Rite of Return, which involves a sorcerer giving a Mummy a task to fulfill. That task is the primary motive for what a Mummy does while on this plane. <br /> <br /> A few of those tasks that have been batted around are 1) Destroying an enemy of the Cult. 2) Protecting the Cult from harm. 3) Finding a relic important to the cult or the god they serve. 4) Escorting someone important to the Mummy or the Cult, whether a member or not. 5) Making a vital decision for the future of the cult, like designating a new leader, etc. 6) Honoring important festivals, gatherings, weddings, funerals, Guild Meetings, Nome Conclaves, and so on.<br /> <br /> I'd like to hear your own tasks you've used in game or just more possibilities a storyteller could use.<br /> <br /> Other reasons for awakening include violation of the tomb- I could see this becoming a Chronicle if the violator was clever, had a method for a fast and easy exit (teleportation spell?), bore evidence of working for a larger conspiracy that might motivate the Mummy to stay awake lest more enemies threaten, and so on. Periods of mass death also awaken an Arisen, although they don't have as strong a purpose as usual. Likewise, Sothic Turns allows for mass risings with little direction to offer. I'm curious if you use any of these non-rite awakenings and how you handle them. <br /> <br /> The other issue that crops up is how important Purpose is to the game, as opposed to the individual player. If all the characters are mummies and they were all summoned to assist with the same purpose, that pretty much answers itself- that's the focus of the game right there. But not all characters in these games are mummies- we have sorcerers, immortals, witnesses, etc. They aren't going to be directly rewarded or punished for following a Mummy's Purpose. There's also the issue of Memory and pursuing goals not related to the Purpose. An easy example is getting diverted by a new relic unrelated to the Call. And there's a question of scope as well. Setting books like Cursed Necropolis DC and Rio focus on big mummy population centers where each undead being has its own agendas, some related to their Purpose and some related to their personal goals. Big Nomes each have Guilds with mummies entering and leaving their orbit as their Descents demand, each one with their own rank and serving a purpose in the Guild in addition to the one their Cults have asked of them (assuming the Cult and Mummy aren't not particularly integrated with the Guild to the point that their goals ARE the Guild's goals...a situation I'd imagine happens most often where Guildmasters and their Cults are involved). <br /> <br /> Also, there's the time travel aspect. Curious how you handle that in your games if you do at all. I personally avoid it even though I like the concept. Do the rest of you prefer to focus on one time period per chronicle? Do you jump between different eras? How do you handle that? Does a mummy enter a death cycle in one era and wake up in another? Does he retain his purpose and memory if that's how you do it (making time travel yet another instance of a potential awakening, in which the original Rite was cast at some point in the past or future and the Mummy is still bound to a task potentially well beyond the era). <br /> <br /> I think I am in the minority when it comes to using Purpose. I like big political games, probably a hold over from my Vampire days. I tend to basically leave the Purpose and the nature of the Call up to the player rather than assign it myself- in the same general spirit that I let a character in another game create his own goals and motives unrelated to the events that will transpire in the Chronicle. The Purposes will be addressed at some point in the middle of the game, while the larger story takes place during and after the task is fulfilled but before the Mummy has drifted back off to Duat. My games usually involve Sorcerers and Immortals as enemies and NPCs rather than characters- I'm not a fan of the power imbalance inherent in Mummies vs the other Players. Wanted to hear your thoughts on the subject.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2244-nothri","name":"nothri","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"485"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-12-03T15:55:10-08:00","id":1469537,"title":"","content":"The Mummy chapter of Mortal Remains summarizes the categories of &quot;mission&quot; a mummy often has as grouped into:<br /> 1. <b>Acquisition of objects of power</b>, i.e. the most standard gameplay loop for Arisen Descents.<br /> 2. <b>Molding cities</b>, which plays nicely with sacred geometry, relic architecture, and other Tomb-adjacent preparation, as well as the greater-scope stuff 2e gestures towards.<br /> 3. <b>Shaping human cultures</b>, which of course plays well with the more high-level directives and projects of the Judges and the Guilds, along with that greater-scope stuff discussed above.<br /> 4. <b>Self-preservation</b>, which extends most directly to the Tomb and the Cult.<br /> 5. <b>Internecine conflict,</b> which 2e feeds nicely through the Judges squabbling for resources.<br /> <br /> It's definitely not exhaustive, but I feel like 2 and 3 bear remembering to keep the gameline's big-picture view in perspective.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}